After exhaustive inspection, the simple_bgp topology is operating correctly in
both directions and there is no fault to report. The BGP session between
router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) over the 193.10.11.0/24 transit link is
fully established and stable. Router1 correctly installs the BGP-learned route
200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2, and router2 correctly installs 195.11.14.0/24
via 193.10.11.1. Both kernel forwarding tables agree with the FRR RIB, and
both host default gateways (pc1 → 195.11.14.1, pc2 → 200.1.1.1) are
correctly pointed at their respective routers. Live probing confirmed 0%
packet loss for ICMP in both directions and a successful 133 Mbps TCP transfer
from pc1 to pc2 with no retransmit anomalies. No iptables or nftables rules
are blocking, rate-limiting, or asymmetrically forwarding any traffic on any
device in the path. The user's report of one-way transfers with replies never
arriving does not match the observed network state. The described symptom —
"replies from pc2 never reach pc1" — is a false premise; the return path is
fully functional. No corrective action is required.
